    {
        "user": "UJBAJNFLK",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1740858997.235199",
        "client_msg_id": "8c28271e-db4b-417a-9046-bf851a52221f",
        "text": "Would a Git repository be sufficient as well? It's no good for chatting, but I much prefer on-demand manual sync (with manual merge) when collaborating on a technical document.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1740781231.434539",
        "parent_user_id": "UE6EFEPTQ",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "qhCn3",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "Would a Git repository be sufficient as well? It's no good for chatting, but I much prefer on-demand manual sync (with manual merge) when collaborating on a technical document."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "U016VUZGUUQ",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1740873138.574189",
        "client_msg_id": "8b2a3c4f-7f93-4e27-8bbd-afb6e173fa88",
        "text": "I call this \"content\" in my notes, but the idea is similar. I have the phrase \"It's the content, stupid\" in there.\n\nThat said, I think it's important to consider what a content/data centric model is like without regard for sync. Sync is cool, but it's also fundamentally unreliable, in the sense of \"the network is unreliable\" as a distributed systems principle, and also maybe I just want to have an offline computer. I don't want sync as a mandatory part of my computing model.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1740781231.434539",
        "parent_user_id": "UE6EFEPTQ",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "6I5u5",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I call this \"content\" in my notes, but the idea is similar. I have the phrase \"It's the content, stupid\" in there.\n\nThat said, I think it's important to consider what a content/data centric model is like without regard for sync. Sync is cool, but it's also fundamentally unreliable, in the sense of \"the network is unreliable\" as a distributed systems principle, and also maybe I just want to have an offline computer. I don't want sync as a mandatory part of my computing model."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "UJBAJNFLK",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1740898896.512909",
        "client_msg_id": "f30f73e7-7dd9-4daf-a06c-709566d5a82c",
        "text": "I wouldn't want sync to be a mandatory part of my computing model either, but I believe that it's a good idea to think about sync when designing a computing model. In particular, sync requires diff and merge, which remain unsolved problems for complex structured data.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1740781231.434539",
        "parent_user_id": "UE6EFEPTQ",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "t2bpg",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I wouldn't want sync to be a mandatory part of my computing model either, but I believe that it's a good idea to think about sync when designing a computing model. In particular, sync requires diff and merge, which remain unsolved problems for complex structured data."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "UE6EFEPTQ",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1740906140.029659",
        "client_msg_id": "4d0a5631-6c7f-45f2-8052-139341314f9a",
        "text": "Well, as I say, at the bottom of sync all you need is packets on the wire, on top of which you can have CRDTs or manual git-like merges, or just last-edit-wins. You can implement reliability or not, it's up to you.\n\nAll I want from a Data-First project is that it does something _other_ than (a) having nothing to say, or (b) offering a silo service locked in to the front end and locking your data in!\n\nSo I simply specifiy \"Local-First\", which covers that in a sufficiently vague way (even though most Local-First folk seem to use CRDTs, that's only a strong suggestion not a requirement as I read it)",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1740781231.434539",
        "parent_user_id": "UE6EFEPTQ",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "KNzeD",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "Well, as I say, at the bottom of sync all you need is packets on the wire, on top of which you can have CRDTs or manual git-like merges, or just last-edit-wins. You can implement reliability or not, it's up to you.\n\nAll I want from a Data-First project is that it does something "
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "other",
                                "style": {
                                    "italic": true
                                }
                            },
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": " than (a) having nothing to say, or (b) offering a silo service locked in to the front end and locking your data in!\n\nSo I simply specifiy \"Local-First\", which covers that in a sufficiently vague way (even though most Local-First folk seem to use CRDTs, that's only a strong suggestion not a requirement as I read it)"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
        "reactions": [
            {
                "name": "+1",
                "users": [
                    "UJBAJNFLK"
